[ar:Just Another Ham Study]
[al:Extra Answers - 2024-2028]
[ti:E9 - Antennas and Transmission Lines]
[00:00.40]Extra answers. E9 - Antennas and Transmission Lines.
[00:05.10]Group E9A.
[00:06.95]What is an isotropic radiator? A hypothetical, lossless antenna having equal radiation intensity in all directions used as a reference for antenna gain.
[00:17.95]What is the effective radiated power (ERP) of a repeater station with 150 watts transmitter power output, 2 dB feed line loss, 2.2 dB duplexer loss, and 7 dBd antenna gain? 286 watts.
[00:34.57]What term describing total radiated power takes into account all gains and losses? Effective radiated power.
[00:42.33]Which of the following factors affect the feed point impedance of an antenna? Antenna height.
[00:47.97]What does the term “ground gain” mean? An increase in signal strength from ground reflections in the environment of the antenna.
[00:55.74]What is the effective radiated power (ERP) of a repeater station with 200 watts transmitter power output, 4 dB feed line loss, 3.2 dB duplexer loss, 0.8 dB circulator loss, and 10 dBd antenna gain? 317 watts.
[01:14.95]What is the effective isotropic radiated power (EIRP) of a repeater station with 200 watts transmitter power output, 2 dB feed line loss, 2.8 dB duplexer loss, 1.2 dB circulator loss, and 7 dBi antenna gain? 252 watts.
[01:34.91]Which frequency band has the smallest first Fresnel zone? 5.8 GHz.
[01:40.46]What is antenna efficiency? Radiation resistance divided by total resistance.
[01:46.49]Which of the following improves the efficiency of a ground-mounted quarter-wave vertical antenna? Installing a ground radial system.
[01:55.16]Which of the following determines ground losses for a ground-mounted vertical antenna operating on HF? Soil conductivity.
[02:03.77]How much gain does an antenna have compared to a half-wavelength dipole if it has 6 dB gain over an isotropic radiator? 3.85 dB.
[02:14.48]Group E9B.
[02:16.37]What is the difference in radiated power between a lossless antenna with gain and an isotropic radiator driven by the same power? They are the same.
[02:26.05]What is the far field of an antenna? The region where the shape of the radiation pattern no longer varies with distance.
[02:33.40]What type of analysis is commonly used for modeling antennas? Method of Moments.
[02:38.87]What is the principle of a Method of Moments analysis? A wire is modeled as a series of segments, each having a uniform value of current.
[02:48.03]What is a disadvantage of decreasing the number of wire segments in an antenna model below 10 segments per half-wavelength? The computed feed point impedance may be incorrect.
[02:59.45]Group E9C.
[03:01.36]What type of radiation pattern is created by two 1/4-wavelength vertical antennas spaced 1/2-wavelength apart and fed 180 degrees out of phase? A figure-eight oriented along the axis of the array.
[03:16.20]What type of radiation pattern is created by two 1/4-wavelength vertical antennas spaced 1/4-wavelength apart and fed 90 degrees out of phase? Cardioid.
[03:27.94]What type of radiation pattern is created by two 1/4-wavelength vertical antennas spaced 1/2-wavelength apart and fed in phase? A figure-eight broadside to the axis of the array.
[03:41.05]What happens to the radiation pattern of an unterminated long wire antenna as the wire length is increased? Additional lobes form with major lobes increasingly aligned with the axis of the antenna.
[03:54.45]What is the purpose of feeding an off-center-fed dipole (OCFD) between the center and one end instead of at the midpoint? To create a similar feed point impedance on multiple bands.
[04:06.45]What is the effect of adding a terminating resistor to a rhombic or long-wire antenna? It changes the radiation pattern from bidirectional to unidirectional.
[04:17.28]What is the approximate feed point impedance at the center of a two-wire half-wave folded dipole antenna? 300 ohms.
[04:25.42]What is a folded dipole antenna? A half-wave dipole with an additional parallel wire connecting its two ends.
[04:32.66]Which of the following describes a G5RV antenna? A wire antenna center-fed through a specific length of open-wire line connected to a balun and coaxial feed line.
[04:44.38]Which of the following describes a Zepp antenna? An end-fed half-wavelength dipole.
[04:49.59]How is the far-field elevation pattern of a vertically polarized antenna affected by being mounted over seawater versus soil? Radiation at low angles increases.
[05:01.29]Which of the following describes an extended double Zepp antenna? A center-fed 1.25-wavelength dipole antenna.
[05:09.47]How does the radiation pattern of a horizontally polarized antenna vary with increasing height above ground? The takeoff angle of the lowest elevation lobe decreases.
[05:20.51]How does the radiation pattern of a horizontally-polarized antenna mounted above a long slope compare with the same antenna mounted above flat ground? The main lobe takeoff angle decreases in the downhill direction.
[05:34.96]Group E9D.
[05:36.85]How much does the gain of an ideal parabolic reflector antenna increase when the operating frequency is doubled? 6 dB.
[05:45.57]How can two linearly polarized Yagi antennas be used to produce circular polarization? Arrange two Yagis on the same axis and perpendicular to each other with the driven elements at the same point on the boom and fed 90 degrees out of phase.
[06:01.70]What is the most efficient location for a loading coil on an electrically short whip? Near the center of the vertical radiator.
[06:09.56]Why should antenna loading coils have a high ratio of reactance to resistance? To maximize efficiency.
[06:16.59]Approximately how long is a Yagi’s driven element? 1/2 wavelength.
[06:21.59]What happens to SWR bandwidth when one or more loading coils are used to resonate an electrically short antenna? It is decreased.
[06:30.54]What is an advantage of top loading an electrically short HF vertical antenna? Improved radiation efficiency.
[06:38.17]What happens as the Q of an antenna increases? SWR bandwidth decreases.
[06:43.70]What is the function of a loading coil in an electrically short antenna? To resonate the antenna by cancelling the capacitive reactance.
[06:52.12]How does radiation resistance of a base-fed whip antenna change below its resonant frequency? Radiation resistance decreases.
[07:00.87]Why do most two-element Yagis with normal spacing have a reflector instead of a director? Higher gain.
[07:07.49]What is the purpose of making a Yagi’s parasitic elements either longer or shorter than resonance? Control of phase shift.
[07:15.50]Group E9E.
[07:17.37]Which matching system for Yagi antennas requires the driven element to be insulated from the boom? Beta or hairpin.
[07:25.34]What antenna matching system matches coaxial cable to an antenna by connecting the shield to the center of the antenna and the conductor a fraction of a wavelength to one side? Gamma match.
[07:37.51]What matching system uses a short length of transmission line connected in parallel with the feed line at or near the feed point? Stub match.
[07:46.54]What is the purpose of the series capacitor in a gamma match? To cancel unwanted inductive reactance.
[07:53.14]What Yagi driven element feed point impedance is required to use a beta or hairpin matching system? Capacitive (driven element electrically shorter than 1/2 wavelength).
[08:05.12]Which of these transmission line impedances would be suitable for constructing a quarter-wave Q-section for matching a 100-ohm feed point impedance to a 50-ohm transmission line? 75 ohms.
[08:18.19]What parameter describes the interaction of a load and transmission line? Reflection coefficient.
[08:24.19]What is a use for a Wilkinson divider? To divide power equally between two 50-ohm loads while maintaining 50-ohm input impedance.
[08:33.35]Which of the following is used to shunt feed a grounded tower at its base? Gamma match.
[08:38.54]What is the purpose of using multiple driven elements connected through phasing lines? To control the antenna’s radiation pattern.
[08:47.02]Group E9F.
[08:48.95]What is the velocity factor of a transmission line? The velocity of a wave in the transmission line divided by the velocity of light in a vacuum.
[08:58.20]Which of the following has the biggest effect on the velocity factor of a transmission line? The insulating dielectric material.
[09:06.08]Why is the electrical length of a coaxial cable longer than its physical length? Electromagnetic waves move more slowly in a coaxial cable than in air.
[09:16.72]What impedance does a 1/2-wavelength transmission line present to an RF generator when the line is shorted at the far end? Very low impedance.
[09:26.71]What is microstrip? Precision printed circuit conductors above a ground plane that provide constant impedance interconnects at microwave frequencies.
[09:36.30]What is the approximate physical length of an air-insulated, parallel conductor transmission line that is electrically 1/2 wavelength long at 14.10 MHz? 10.6 meters.
[09:49.75]How does parallel conductor transmission line compare to coaxial cable with a plastic dielectric? Lower loss.
[09:57.14]Which of the following is a significant difference between foam dielectric coaxial cable and solid dielectric coaxial cable, assuming all other parameters are the same? Foam dielectric coaxial cable has lower safe maximum operating voltage; Foam dielectric coaxial cable has lower loss per unit of length; Foam dielectric coaxial cable has higher velocity factor; All these choices are correct.
[10:23.15]What impedance does a 1/4-wavelength transmission line present to an RF generator when the line is shorted at the far end? Very high impedance.
[10:33.23]What impedance does a 1/8-wavelength transmission line present to an RF generator when the line is shorted at the far end? An inductive reactance.
[10:43.50]What impedance does a 1/8-wavelength transmission line present to an RF generator when the line is open at the far end? A capacitive reactance.
[10:53.84]What impedance does a 1/4-wavelength transmission line present to an RF generator when the line is open at the far end? Very low impedance.
[11:04.34]Group E9G.
[11:06.29]Which of the following can be calculated using a Smith chart? Impedance along transmission lines.
[11:12.32]What type of coordinate system is used in a Smith chart? Resistance circles and reactance arcs.
[11:18.62]Which of the following is often determined using a Smith chart? Impedance and SWR values in transmission lines.
[11:26.48]What are the two families of circles and arcs that make up a Smith chart? Resistance and reactance.
[11:32.51]Which of the following is a common use for a Smith chart? Determine the length and position of an impedance matching stub.
[11:39.83]How is a Smith chart normalized? Reassign the prime center’s impedance value.
[11:45.05]What third family of circles is often added to a Smith chart during the process of designing impedance matching networks? Constant-SWR circles.
[11:55.15]What do the arcs on a Smith chart represent? Points with constant reactance.
[11:59.83]In what units are the wavelength scales on a Smith chart calibrated? In fractions of transmission line electrical wavelength.
[12:08.47]Group E9H.
[12:10.45]When constructing a Beverage antenna, which of the following factors should be included in the design to achieve good performance at the desired frequency? It should be at least one wavelength long.
[12:22.28]Which is generally true for 160- and 80-meter receiving antennas? Atmospheric noise is so high that directivity is much more important than losses.
[12:33.04]What is receiving directivity factor (RDF)? Peak antenna gain compared to average gain over the hemisphere around and above the antenna.
[12:42.21]What is the purpose of placing an electrostatic shield around a small-loop direction-finding antenna? It eliminates unbalanced capacitive coupling to the antenna’s surroundings, improving the depth of its nulls.
[12:55.68]What challenge is presented by a small wire-loop antenna for direction finding? It has a bidirectional null pattern.
[13:02.92]What indicates the correct value of terminating resistance for a Beverage antenna? Minimum variation in SWR over the desired frequency range.
[13:13.19]What is the function of a Beverage antenna’s termination resistor? Absorb signals from the reverse direction.
[13:20.28]What is the function of a sense antenna? It modifies the pattern of a DF antenna to provide a null in only one direction.
[13:28.48]What type of radiation pattern is created by a single-turn, terminated loop such as a pennant antenna? Cardioid.
[13:36.00]How can the output voltage of a multiple-turn receiving loop antenna be increased? By increasing the number of turns and/or the area enclosed by the loop.
[13:46.12]What feature of a cardioid pattern antenna makes it useful for direction-finding antennas? A single null.
